# Working on a Loom app

Loom is new: don't guess its syntax or API. Read the docs, then let the
checker correct you. The `loom` MCP server (`.mcp.json`, `loom mcp`)
gives every tool below; each returns the JSON of `loom ... --json`.

## 1. Learn before writing

- `docs` tool (`loom docs TOPIC`): read `syntax` and `live` before your
  first template; `layouts`, `components`, `data`, `jobs` when the task
  touches them. With no topic it lists them in reading order.
- `components`: the starter kit. Add one with `loom components add NAME`
  rather than writing your own button, input or dialog.
- `routes` and `manifest` (app running): what the app already has.
- `db_schema` before writing SQL; `migrate_status` to see migrations.

## 2. Edit

A page is `name.go` (state, `Mount`, `Assigns`, `Events`) next to `name.hx` (its
component), routed in `Setup` in `app.go`. Never edit `*_hx.go`.

## 3. Check

`check` tool (`loom check --json`). An empty list means every template
is valid. Otherwise apply each diagnostic's `fix`; `docs` with topic
`errors` explains each code. Repeat until clean.

## 4. Build

`build` tool (`loom build`): checks again and regenerates `*_hx.go`. Do
this before any `go build` or `go test`: a stale `*_hx.go` still
compiles. Add starter kit components with `components_add`. Apply new
migrations with `migrate`.

## 5. Test

`test` tool (`loom test --json`: build, then `go test ./...`, with each
failed test's output). Write page tests with `loomtest.Mount`,
`p.Click(selector)`, `p.Submit(selector, fields)` and `p.AssertHTML(selector, n)`
(`docs` topic `live`).

## 6. Verify in the running app

With `loom dev ./cmd/{{.Command}}` running (it rebuilds on save):

- `dev_status` with `wait: true` after saving: whether the rebuild
  worked, and the errors if not.
- `recent_errors`: runtime errors with codes and fixes, from the server
  and the browser. Check after every change.
- `sessions`, then `inspect_session`: a page's assigns and HTML.
- `send_event`: click or submit on an open page (`sid`, `event`,
  `payload`) and get the new HTML and any errors back.
- `render_component`: render a component with sample assigns.
- `previews`, `preview_screenshot`: see what a component looks like,
  light and dark.
- `db_query`: read the data a page shows (read-only).
- `jobs_list`, `queues`, `cron`: background jobs.

Done means: `check` is clean, tests pass, and `recent_errors` is empty
after using the page.
